course: dictionary
lesson: 4724
title: Hopelessly: two meanings in one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Hopelessly» can mean two things. The first is sad and without hope: you think things cannot get better. The second is completely or very much, and it is usually about something bad. Read the words around it to decide which meaning fits.
explain: In «Hopelessly lost, they sit on a fallen log», the clue is lost. Nobody is hoping to be found, so the word means completely lost. In «The cart: axles hopelessly bent», the bent axles are beyond repair, so hopelessly means badly.
explain: Try the swap check. Put «completely» in place of «hopelessly»: «completely lost» still makes sense, so the second meaning is right. Story writers use this word a lot, as in «hopelessly in love», to show how strong a feeling or situation is.
fact: «Hopelessly» is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens or how far something goes.
fact: Meaning 1: without hope, in a way that shows you think things cannot get better.
fact: Meaning 2: completely or very much, usually about something bad: «hopelessly lost», «hopelessly outnumbered».
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Mole is hopelessly lost in the Wild Wood in the snow until Ratty finds him.
fact: «Well might the ferrets rush wildly for the fireplace and get hopelessly jammed in the chimney!»
exercise: «Hopelessly lost, they sit on a fallen log.» What does hopelessly mean here? | Quickly, with great speed | Lost for a short time only | *Completely lost, with no way to find the path | Happily lost, as on a game
exercise: «...and get hopelessly jammed in the chimney!» What does hopelessly mean here? | Gently and softly | Just a little bit | With hope and joy | *Completely stuck
exercise: «axles hopelessly bent» in the cart story suggests the axles are what? | Painted bright | *Badly bent, beyond repair | Slightly curved and easy to fix | Hopefully straight
test: Mole is ___ lost in the Wild Wood in the snow. Which word fits best? | *hopelessly | quickly | happily | barely
test: Which word tells you a person thinks things cannot get better? | snow | lost | *hopelessly | willows
exam: Which phrase uses «hopelessly» to mean completely? | Ratty found Mole in the snow | Mole felt sleepy by the fire | *hopelessly lost in the Wild Wood | The river ran quickly past the willows
